  "cards": [
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.097.c1",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "We can’t visit the stars. So how do we know so much about them?"
    },
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.097.c2",
      "type": "comparison",
      "headline": "Discovery ← method",
      "items": [
        {
          "label": "What stars are made of",
          "points": [
            "Spectral lines in their light"
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Distance to nearby stars",
          "points": [
            "Parallax as Earth orbits"
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Planets around other stars",
          "points": [
            "Transits and wobbles"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.097.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "New windows",
      "body": "Some discoveries needed entirely new signals. Stars at galaxies’ edges orbiting too fast revealed dark matter. Redshift in galaxies’ spectra revealed the expanding universe. And LIGO first detected gravitational waves in 2015, from merging black holes."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.097.c4",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Old tricks still work",
      "body": "Some methods needed no telescope at all. Around 240 BCE, Eratosthenes estimated Earth’s size from the Sun’s noon angle in two cities. Careful measurement and reasoning, checked by others, is how astronomy has always worked."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.astronomy.097.c5",
